# The Myth of the Two Springs

In the beginning, there were two villages separated by a barren valley. Each village had a sacred spring that provided exactly five measures of water each day to one chosen villager.

The first day, a villager from the Eastern village carried her water to the valley's center. As she poured it onto the dry earth, a miracle occurred—the water multiplied threefold, creating a small pool. She called to her Western neighbor, who decided how much of this abundant pool to carry back as gratitude.

The next day, they switched roles. The Western villager now carried his five measures, which again tripled in the valley. The Eastern villager chose her portion of thanks.

And so it continued, day after day, each taking turns as giver and receiver. When both trusted fully and shared generously, the valley bloomed. When either grew suspicious or greedy, the earth cracked and thirsted.

The elders taught: "Abundance flows from trust. What you pour out returns multiplied, but only if both hearts remain open. For the magic lies not in the water alone, but in the covenant between givers who take turns holding faith."

The valley remembers every measure given, and every measure returned.
